WEBVTT

00:00:00.987 --> 00:00:03.585
This is a frog from the CIFAR-10 test set.

00:00:03.585 --> 00:00:05.585
We wanted a neural network that could look at images

00:00:05.585 --> 00:00:07.454
like this and say what's in them,

00:00:07.454 --> 00:00:10.861
even though it never really sees a picture, it only sees numbers.

00:00:11.068 --> 00:00:15.275
CIFAR-10 has 60,000 tiny color photos split across ten classes,

00:00:15.275 --> 00:00:18.612
things like airplanes, cats, frogs, and trucks.

00:00:18.612 --> 00:00:20.960
The official split gives us 50,000 images to

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.931
train on and keeps 10,000 aside for testing.

00:00:24.139 --> 00:00:26.736
If you zoom in on the frog, it's really just a 32 by

00:00:26.736 --> 00:00:30.204
32 grid of pixels, and each pixel is three numbers between

00:00:30.204 --> 00:00:35.128
0 and 255 for how much red, green, and blue it has.

00:00:35.128 --> 00:00:41.044
So every image is 32 times 32 times 3, which is 3,072 numbers.

00:00:41.343 --> 00:00:44.441
The rest of this goes through the experiments we ran to figure out what

00:00:44.441 --> 00:00:47.942
makes a network better at turning those numbers into the right label.

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:52.230
The network we started with is a small convolutional

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.936
network from class, with 545,098 trainable parameters,

00:00:56.936 --> 00:00:59.145
which are the numbers it adjusts while it learns.

00:00:59.355 --> 00:01:03.735
Before anything else, every pixel value gets divided by 255,

00:01:03.735 --> 00:01:07.018
and then each color channel has its dataset average subtracted and gets

00:01:07.018 --> 00:01:10.050
divided by its standard deviation, which puts the channels on a

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:13.778
similar scale around zero and usually makes training better behaved.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.657
The first real layer is a convolution.

00:01:16.657 --> 00:01:20.375
A filter is a 3 by 3 window over all three color channels,

00:01:20.375 --> 00:01:22.918
so it has 27 weights plus a bias.

00:01:22.918 --> 00:01:25.364
At each position it multiplies the values under it

00:01:25.364 --> 00:01:27.788
by its weights and adds everything up,

00:01:27.788 --> 00:01:30.951
and this panel is that exact calculation from our trained model.

00:01:30.951 --> 00:01:32.603
Slide the same window across every

00:01:32.603 --> 00:01:35.990
position and you get a new 32 by 32 map.

00:01:36.275 --> 00:01:39.101
The filter uses the same weights at every position,

00:01:39.101 --> 00:01:42.993
so a pattern it learns in one spot can be picked up anywhere else in the image.

00:01:42.993 --> 00:01:47.914
That's also why all 32 filters in this layer only need 896 parameters.

00:01:48.117 --> 00:01:50.509
These are the actual filters our baseline learned,

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:52.498
and the maps they produce for this frog.

00:01:52.498 --> 00:01:55.927
Nobody designed these weights by hand, training found all of them.

00:01:56.213 --> 00:02:00.061
After the convolution, ReLU replaces every negative number with zero,

00:02:00.061 --> 00:02:03.626
and then 2 by 2 max pooling keeps only the biggest value in each

00:02:03.626 --> 00:02:09.111
2 by 2 block, which cuts the maps from 32 by 32 down to 16 by 16.

00:02:09.316 --> 00:02:12.393
The second block does the same thing with 64 filters,

00:02:12.393 --> 00:02:16.001
but now each filter reads all 32 maps from the first block,

00:02:16.001 --> 00:02:19.208
so it can combine simpler patterns into more complicated ones.

00:02:19.208 --> 00:02:21.891
Pooling then takes everything down to 8 by 8.

00:02:22.083 --> 00:02:27.072
That leaves 64 maps of 8 by 8, which is 4,096 numbers.

00:02:27.072 --> 00:02:32.083
We flatten them into one long list, pass it through a hidden layer of 128 units,

00:02:32.083 --> 00:02:35.436
and the last layer gives us ten scores, one for each class.

00:02:37.313 --> 00:02:39.139
Those ten scores are called logits.

00:02:39.139 --> 00:02:41.846
They can be any size, positive or negative,

00:02:41.846 --> 00:02:44.008
so on their own they aren't probabilities yet.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.144
Softmax is what turns them into probabilities.

00:02:47.144 --> 00:02:50.666
You exponentiate each score, which makes everything positive,

00:02:50.666 --> 00:02:53.603
and then divide by the total so they all add up to one.

00:02:54.384 --> 00:02:57.608
Here the model puts about 74 percent on frog and about

00:02:57.608 --> 00:03:01.009
14 percent on cat, so it's mostly right but not very sure.

00:03:01.128 --> 00:03:04.222
To train it, we need one number for how wrong it was,

00:03:04.222 --> 00:03:06.227
and that's the cross-entropy loss,

00:03:06.227 --> 00:03:09.836
the negative log of the probability it gave the correct class.

00:03:09.836 --> 00:03:12.243
Confident and right costs almost nothing,

00:03:12.243 --> 00:03:14.133
this frog only costs about 0.31.

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:16.024
Confident and wrong costs a lot,

00:03:16.024 --> 00:03:19.633
so giving the right answer just 2 percent would cost about 3.9.

00:03:20.498 --> 00:03:22.303
One detail about PyTorch here.

00:03:22.303 --> 00:03:25.944
The model itself only outputs logits, and PyTorch's cross-entropy

00:03:25.944 --> 00:03:29.346
loss applies log-softmax internally, so softmax is just

00:03:29.346 --> 00:03:32.724
how we read the outputs, it isn't an extra layer in the model.

00:03:33.007 --> 00:03:35.910
Backpropagation then uses the chain rule to work backwards

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.323
from the loss through every layer, and gives each

00:03:38.323 --> 00:03:42.529
of the 545,098 parameters a gradient, which says how the

00:03:42.529 --> 00:03:45.702
loss would change if that parameter moved a tiny bit.

00:03:46.473 --> 00:03:50.332
Gradient descent moves every weight a small step against its gradient,

00:03:50.332 --> 00:03:52.614
with the learning rate setting the step size.

00:03:52.614 --> 00:03:56.386
This surface is a real slice of the loss around our trained baseline,

00:03:56.386 --> 00:03:59.745
along two random directions in weight space, and the dashed path

00:03:59.745 --> 00:04:03.475
is just an illustration of stepping downhill, not steps we recorded.

00:04:04.254 --> 00:04:08.047
Each step uses a batch of 64 images, so one full pass through the

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:13.072
50,000 training images, which is called an epoch, takes 782 steps.

00:04:13.172 --> 00:04:16.320
We trained for ten epochs with a learning rate of 0.01,

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.411
and this baseline reached 63.68 percent test accuracy.

00:04:19.411 --> 00:04:23.064
That's a lot better than the 10 percent you'd get from guessing,

00:04:23.064 --> 00:04:26.493
but it's still wrong on more than a third of the test images.

00:04:28.341 --> 00:04:31.550
The first thing we tried was making training faster.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:34.456
At batch size 64 the GPU was only busy about 42

00:04:34.456 --> 00:04:37.422
percent of the time, so we tried batch size 512,

00:04:37.422 --> 00:04:40.510
and ten epochs finished in 5 seconds instead of 22.

00:04:40.607 --> 00:04:44.204
The catch was that test accuracy dropped to 48.58 percent.

00:04:44.204 --> 00:04:48.472
With the same number of epochs, a batch eight times bigger also means

00:04:48.472 --> 00:04:53.471
eight times fewer weight updates, 980 instead of 7,820 at the same learning rate,

00:04:53.471 --> 00:04:57.312
so our guess was that the model just hadn't taken enough steps.

00:04:57.508 --> 00:05:00.870
To check that, we trained batch 512 for 80 epochs,

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:04.232
which gives 7,840 updates, almost exactly the same

00:05:04.232 --> 00:05:07.725
as the baseline, and that run reached 67.67 percent.

00:05:07.725 --> 00:05:12.669
That fits the step count explanation, but it isn't a perfectly clean test,

00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:16.756
because this run also went through eight times as many images.

00:05:16.861 --> 00:05:20.714
So with matched steps, batch 512 ended up about 4 points higher,

00:05:20.714 --> 00:05:22.966
and it took 37 seconds instead of 22.

00:05:22.966 --> 00:05:25.456
These are single runs with one seed each,

00:05:25.456 --> 00:05:28.598
so the exact gap isn't something to lean on too hard.

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:33.834
Next we tried data augmentation, which means every time the network

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:37.454
sees a training image, it gets a randomly changed version of it.

00:05:37.454 --> 00:05:39.280
Flip it left to right half the time,

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.356
or pad the border with 4 black pixels and crop a random 32 by 32 window,

00:05:44.356 --> 00:05:46.188
which shifts the image around a little.

00:05:46.383 --> 00:05:49.894
A horse facing left is still a horse, so the label stays the same,

00:05:49.894 --> 00:05:52.318
and the hope is that the network learns to ignore things

00:05:52.318 --> 00:05:55.633
like direction and small shifts, which is called invariance.

00:05:55.633 --> 00:05:59.405
Seeing varied views of each image also makes it harder to just memorize

00:05:59.405 --> 00:06:03.073
the exact training images, so it works as a kind of regularization.

00:06:03.193 --> 00:06:06.672
We tested 15 different recipes on the small network

00:06:06.672 --> 00:06:09.884
with three seeds each, measuring test accuracy.

00:06:09.884 --> 00:06:12.226
No augmentation got 67.96 percent.

00:06:12.226 --> 00:06:16.776
Flip on its own helped a little, 68.77, and it improved every seed.

00:06:16.776 --> 00:06:19.787
Crop plus flip actually hurt, down to 66.38,

00:06:19.787 --> 00:06:24.337
and everything we added on top of it did worse than no augmentation.

00:06:24.626 --> 00:06:27.528
Then there's the gap between training and test accuracy,

00:06:27.528 --> 00:06:31.963
which went from about 7 points without augmentation to under 1 with crop plus flip.

00:06:31.963 --> 00:06:34.734
A smaller gap can mean useful regularization,

00:06:34.734 --> 00:06:38.680
or it can just mean the model learned less, and since test accuracy went down,

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.854
the gap alone doesn't show an improvement.

00:06:40.854 --> 00:06:43.941
Our best guess is that this small network on a short recipe

00:06:43.941 --> 00:06:46.704
couldn't fit the harder data, but we didn't test that.

00:06:47.004 --> 00:06:49.830
This suite also looked at the test set over and over,

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:51.539
so these results are exploratory.

00:06:53.478 --> 00:06:56.173
Because of that, we got stricter about evaluation.

00:06:56.173 --> 00:06:59.402
We took 5,000 images out of the training set as a stratified

00:06:59.402 --> 00:07:04.130
validation set, 500 from each class, trained on the other 45,000,

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:06.836
and made every decision on validation from then on.

00:07:06.836 --> 00:07:09.380
The test set was reserved for a final check of whichever

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:12.836
model got selected, although it isn't a pristine holdout anymore,

00:07:12.836 --> 00:07:14.871
since the earlier suite already used it.

00:07:15.161 --> 00:07:19.161
We also switched to a stronger training recipe: batch 128,

00:07:19.161 --> 00:07:22.596
SGD with momentum and weight decay, batch norm after

00:07:22.596 --> 00:07:26.237
every convolution, and a learning rate that warms up for 5 epochs,

00:07:26.237 --> 00:07:29.585
then follows a cosine curve down over 160 epochs.

00:07:29.585 --> 00:07:32.367
Momentum smooths out the steps, weight decay keeps

00:07:32.367 --> 00:07:34.900
the weights small, and batch norm rescales each

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:37.984
layer's outputs so deeper networks train more easily.

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:41.289
Every setup also ran with three different random seeds.

00:07:41.289 --> 00:07:43.974
A run's score is its average validation accuracy over the

00:07:43.974 --> 00:07:46.692
last ten epochs instead of its single best epoch,

00:07:46.692 --> 00:07:50.219
and the plus or minus is how much that score varies across seeds.

00:07:50.418 --> 00:07:54.813
With this setup, the same two-convolution design plus batch norm scores

00:07:54.813 --> 00:07:59.146
78.27 percent on validation, compared to 63.68 percent on test before.

00:07:59.146 --> 00:08:02.870
But the split, the metric, the epochs, and the optimizer all

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:06.715
changed at once, so we can't say which change caused that jump.

00:08:08.663 --> 00:08:11.250
Next we wanted to know if more convolutions would help.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:15.163
We kept the same two stages, the same pooling, and the same classifier,

00:08:15.163 --> 00:08:19.617
and only stacked more convolutions inside each stage, from 2 up to 32.

00:08:19.821 --> 00:08:24.266
Each extra 3 by 3 layer lets a unit see a slightly bigger part of the image,

00:08:24.266 --> 00:08:27.897
5 by 5 after two layers, then 7 by 7 after three,

00:08:27.897 --> 00:08:31.327
and it gives the network more steps to build up complicated features.

00:08:31.511 --> 00:08:35.011
On validation, two convolutions got 78.27,

00:08:35.011 --> 00:08:41.402
four got 84.53, eight got 86.90, and sixteen got 87.17,

00:08:41.402 --> 00:08:43.706
so the gains were shrinking but still positive.

00:08:43.706 --> 00:08:47.456
Then at 32 convolutions it dropped to 83.08,

00:08:47.456 --> 00:08:49.257
and all three seeds got worse.

00:08:49.535 --> 00:08:52.243
Every depth reached 100 percent training accuracy,

00:08:52.243 --> 00:08:55.324
so the 32 layer network could still fit the training set,

00:08:55.324 --> 00:08:59.520
it just did worse on images it hadn't seen, and these runs alone can't tell us

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.759
whether that came from optimization, generalization, or both.

00:09:03.055 --> 00:09:05.229
That's different from the original ResNet paper,

00:09:05.229 --> 00:09:07.892
where deeper plain networks had higher training error.

00:09:07.892 --> 00:09:10.098
Ours trained fine and fell on validation.

00:09:10.098 --> 00:09:12.468
Residual connections are the standard fix for deep

00:09:12.468 --> 00:09:15.479
plain networks, so they were a natural next thing to try,

00:09:15.479 --> 00:09:17.214
even though we didn't know the cause.

00:09:19.068 --> 00:09:22.861
A residual block keeps the same two convolutions, but adds a shortcut

00:09:22.861 --> 00:09:26.421
that carries the block's input around them and adds it back at the end.

00:09:26.606 --> 00:09:31.279
So the main path computes F of x, which is conv, batch norm, ReLU,

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:35.903
conv, batch norm, and the block outputs ReLU of F of x plus the shortcut.

00:09:36.769 --> 00:09:39.932
When the number of channels doubles from 32 to 64,

00:09:39.932 --> 00:09:43.867
our shortcut just pads the input with 32 channels of zeros.

00:09:43.867 --> 00:09:46.639
That adds no learned weights, so every residual network

00:09:46.639 --> 00:09:49.965
has exactly the same number of parameters as its plain version,

00:09:49.965 --> 00:09:52.235
and the two start from the same initial weights.

00:09:52.437 --> 00:09:56.079
The usual explanation is that if a block should barely change its input,

00:09:56.079 --> 00:09:59.274
a plain block has to learn to copy it through two convolutions,

00:09:59.274 --> 00:10:02.176
while a residual block just pushes F toward zero.

00:10:02.176 --> 00:10:03.992
During backprop, the shortcuts also

00:10:03.992 --> 00:10:06.242
give the gradient a direct path back.

00:10:06.242 --> 00:10:08.708
These pictures are schematics, not measurements.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:14.414
Residual 4 got 83.77 on validation, eight got 86.67,

00:10:14.414 --> 00:10:19.200
sixteen got 86.85, and thirty-two got 87.20.

00:10:19.299 --> 00:10:22.677
So at 32 layers the shortcuts clearly mattered,

00:10:22.677 --> 00:10:24.787
about 4 points over plain 32.

00:10:24.787 --> 00:10:29.783
But residual didn't beat plain at 4, 8, or 16, and residual 32 is only

00:10:29.783 --> 00:10:34.638
0.03 points above plain 16, well inside the variation between seeds.

00:10:34.638 --> 00:10:38.437
It still had the highest average, so it was selected,

00:10:38.437 --> 00:10:42.940
and on the test set it scored 86.43 percent, plus or minus 0.49.

00:10:44.893 --> 00:10:47.197
Since crop plus flip hurt the small network,

00:10:47.197 --> 00:10:51.262
we tried it again on residual 32, keeping the recipe, the seeds,

00:10:51.262 --> 00:10:54.683
and the starting weights the same, and only changing the augmentation.

00:10:54.887 --> 00:10:58.496
On validation, no augmentation got 87.20,

00:10:58.496 --> 00:11:03.398
flip got 90.31, and crop plus flip got 93.74.

00:11:03.398 --> 00:11:08.097
Crop plus flip improved all three seeds, by roughly 6 to 7 points each.

00:11:08.385 --> 00:11:10.244
So the same augmentation that cost the small

00:11:10.244 --> 00:11:14.624
network about 1.6 points gave residual 32 about 6.5.

00:11:14.624 --> 00:11:17.320
Our best guess is that a bigger network with a longer,

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:20.374
stronger recipe has room to learn from the extra variety.

00:11:20.374 --> 00:11:23.352
We didn't prove that though, since the model, the recipe,

00:11:23.352 --> 00:11:26.072
and the split all changed between the two experiments.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.511
Last, we tried ResNet-18, which is a much bigger residual network,

00:11:32.511 --> 00:11:35.228
adapted for CIFAR and trained from random weights,

00:11:35.228 --> 00:11:37.202
so there's no pre-training on ImageNet.

00:11:37.418 --> 00:11:42.081
The standard version starts with a 7 by 7 stride 2 convolution and a max pool,

00:11:42.081 --> 00:11:44.266
which is built for big ImageNet photos and would

00:11:44.266 --> 00:11:47.668
shrink our images to 8 by 8 before the blocks even start,

00:11:47.668 --> 00:11:52.201
so we swapped in one 3 by 3 stride 1 convolution and removed the max pool.

00:11:52.391 --> 00:11:54.880
After that come eight basic residual blocks,

00:11:54.880 --> 00:12:00.978
two per stage, with 64, 128, 256, and 512 channels.

00:12:00.978 --> 00:12:04.586
The first stage stays at 32 by 32, and each of the next three

00:12:04.586 --> 00:12:08.989
halves the resolution, so the maps go from 32 to 16 to 8 to 4.

00:12:08.989 --> 00:12:11.576
Where the shape changes, the shortcut is a learned 1 by

00:12:11.576 --> 00:12:14.865
1 convolution with batch norm instead of zero padding.

00:12:14.983 --> 00:12:18.965
At the end, global average pooling turns each 4 by 4 map into

00:12:18.965 --> 00:12:22.882
a single number, and one linear layer maps those 512 numbers

00:12:22.882 --> 00:12:26.864
to the ten classes, with no 128 unit hidden layer like before.

00:12:27.064 --> 00:12:30.706
The 18 comes from counting the layers with weights along the main path.

00:12:30.706 --> 00:12:33.966
The stem is one, the blocks have 16 convolutions,

00:12:33.966 --> 00:12:35.695
and the classifier makes 18.

00:12:35.695 --> 00:12:38.450
The projection shortcuts aren't counted, by convention.

00:12:38.747 --> 00:12:42.660
So compared with residual 32, a lot more changed than the layer count.

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:45.834
ResNet-18 keeps higher resolution for longer,

00:12:45.834 --> 00:12:49.497
has many more channels, learned shortcuts, a global pooling head,

00:12:49.497 --> 00:12:53.563
and about 11.2 million parameters, roughly nine times as many.

00:12:53.563 --> 00:12:56.813
It also trained for 200 epochs instead of 160,

00:12:56.813 --> 00:12:59.986
so this compares whole recipes, not just architectures.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:04.498
Across three seeds, it averaged 95.77 percent validation

00:13:04.498 --> 00:13:08.664
accuracy over the last ten epochs, plus or minus 0.11,

00:13:08.664 --> 00:13:13.285
compared to 93.74 for residual 32 with the same augmentation.

00:13:15.143 --> 00:13:19.753
When the queue finished, it picked ResNet-18 with crop plus flip based

00:13:19.753 --> 00:13:24.167
on validation, and only then did we run that model on the test set.

00:13:24.167 --> 00:13:26.505
It got 95.21 percent test accuracy,

00:13:26.505 --> 00:13:29.232
plus or minus 0.02 across the three seeds.

00:13:29.522 --> 00:13:34.315
Putting the whole path together, the classroom baseline got 63.68 on test,

00:13:34.315 --> 00:13:37.956
and then on validation the stricter recipe got 78.27,

00:13:37.956 --> 00:13:46.380
depth and shortcuts got 87.20, crop and flip got 93.74, and ResNet-18 got 95.77.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:48.696
The metrics and setups changed along the way,

00:13:48.696 --> 00:13:51.098
so these numbers show the order things happened in,

00:13:51.098 --> 00:13:53.497
not a leaderboard, and they shouldn't be added up.

00:13:53.781 --> 00:13:56.400
This is the strongest result we measured in this project,

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:58.346
and it's not the best ResNet out there.

00:13:58.346 --> 00:14:01.531
Wider and deeper residual networks and other training recipes

00:14:01.531 --> 00:14:05.564
get higher accuracy on CIFAR-10, so this isn't state of the art.

00:14:05.833 --> 00:14:08.888
If we did this again, we'd keep comparing runs by optimizer

00:14:08.888 --> 00:14:12.822
steps and not just epochs, deciding on validation across seeds,

00:14:12.822 --> 00:14:16.322
not treating a smaller train-test gap as an improvement by itself,

00:14:16.322 --> 00:14:19.333
and retesting a technique when the model or recipe changes,

00:14:19.333 --> 00:14:21.803
since crop plus flip went from hurting to helping.

00:14:21.995 --> 00:14:25.325
Going from 63.68 to 95.21 percent on test took a lot

00:14:25.325 --> 00:14:28.529
of changes stacked together, and these experiments

00:14:28.529 --> 00:14:31.859
don't split up exactly how much each one contributed.
